In Hawaii, handgun buyers are living with two rules a federal appeals court has just kept in force,

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:45.560
the Garden Island reports. One is 30 days to use a permit to acquire a handgun.

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:50.500
The other requires that some newly acquired guns be brought to a police station for inspection.

00:03:51.220 --> 00:03:55.500
The U.S. Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit upheld both in a six-to-five opinion.

00:03:55.980 --> 00:03:58.820
The State Department of the Attorney General announced the result.

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:02.440
The strongest case for keeping the rules is the one the majority wrote.

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:08.320
Hawaii, the ruling explains, is a shall issue state, an applicant who satisfies objective

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:14.080
requirements may obtain a permit. Guided by the US Supreme Court's 2022 decision in New

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:19.520
York State Rifle and Pistol Association vs. Bruin, the on-bomb court held that this system

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:24.000
fits the Second Amendment. The majority said the plaintiffs failed to show that the two

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:29.360
provisions, which the majority called presumptively lawful, have been put toward abusive ends that

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:31.960
it would effectively deny ordinary citizens their rights.

00:04:32.600 --> 00:04:38.540
The strongest case against is the lawsuit Todd Yukotaki and David Kikukawa filed in 2019.

00:04:39.140 --> 00:04:41.940
They challenge the narrow window as unconstitutional.

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:47.600
They also challenge to form asking for information about the firearm, and they challenge the duty

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:52.040
